---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/aspe/aspe1100.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "1. Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "2. Scope — Establishing What Counts as GAAP" },
        { id: "s3", label: "3. The Primary Source — Part II of the CPA Canada Handbook" },
        { id: "s4", label: "4. Where a Specific Section Directly Addresses the Issue" },
        { id: "s5", label: "5. Where No Section Directly Applies — Returning to Section 1000 First" },
        { id: "s6", label: "6. Permitted Secondary Sources — Looking to Other Frameworks by Analogy" },
        { id: "s7", label: "7. Accepted Industry Practice as a Legitimate Reference Point" },
        { id: "s8", label: "8. Worked Example — Applying the Hierarchy to an Unaddressed Transaction" },
        { id: "s9", label: "9. Worked Example — Choosing Between Two Permitted Secondary Sources" },
        { id: "s10", label: "10. Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s11", label: "11. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s12", label: "12. Relationship to IAS 8's GAAP Hierarchy" },
        { id: "s13", label: "13. Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "14. Related Standards" },
        { id: "s15", label: "15. Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">
</div>



<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A dedicated, standalone hierarchy for filling gaps</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 1100 ESTABLISHES what CONSTITUTES Canadian generally ACCEPTED accounting PRINCIPLES for PRIVATE enterprises, and SETS OUT the HIERARCHY of sources an ENTITY consults WHEN a SPECIFIC transaction or EVENT is NOT directly ADDRESSED by an EXISTING ASPE Section. UNLIKE IFRS (WHERE this TOPIC is FOLDED into IAS 8'S broader ACCOUNTING policies discussion), ASPE gives THIS hierarchy ITS OWN dedicated, STANDALONE Section.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Establishing What Counts as GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Defining the authoritative source</div><div class="principle">This Section applies WHENEVER a PRIVATE enterprise PREPARES financial STATEMENTS in ACCORDANCE with Canadian GAAP as SET OUT in PART II of the CPA CANADA Handbook — DEFINING the SOURCES an ENTITY may LEGITIMATELY rely UPON in FORMING its ACCOUNTING policies.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Primary Source — Part II of the CPA Canada Handbook</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">The individual ASPE Sections themselves</div><div class="text-supporting">The PRIMARY source of GAAP for a PRIVATE enterprise is the INDIVIDUAL Sections COMPRISING Part II of the CPA CANADA Handbook — the ASPE SECTIONS covered THROUGHOUT this poster SERIES. WHERE a SPECIFIC Section directly ADDRESSES a TRANSACTION or EVENT, THAT Section'S guidance GOVERNS, WITHOUT need to CONSULT any OTHER source.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Where a Specific Section Directly Addresses the Issue</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">No need to look further</div><div class="text-supporting">IF an EXISTING ASPE Section DIRECTLY addresses a PARTICULAR transaction or EVENT, an ENTITY simply APPLIES that SECTION'S guidance — THE hierarchy discussed BELOW (Sections 5-6) becomes RELEVANT only WHERE genuine GAPS exist.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Where No Section Directly Applies — Returning to Section 1000 First</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">The conceptual framework as the first port of call</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE NO specific ASPE Section DIRECTLY addresses a TRANSACTION or EVENT, an ENTITY FIRST returns to SECTION 1000 (FINANCIAL Statement Concepts — see the COMPANION poster) — APPLYING the GENERAL recognition CRITERIA, measurement BASES, and QUALITATIVE characteristics DISCUSSED there to DEVELOP an APPROPRIATE accounting POLICY.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Permitted Secondary Sources — Looking to Other Frameworks by Analogy</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A genuinely distinctive, explicit invitation to borrow from other frameworks</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE Section 1000 ALONE does NOT resolve the ISSUE, an ENTITY MAY consider ACCOUNTING treatments in OTHER sources DEALING with SIMILAR or RELATED issues — INCLUDING Part I of the HANDBOOK (Canadian-ADOPTED IFRS), OTHER established ACCOUNTING frameworks (SUCH as US GAAP), and OTHER accounting LITERATURE — a GENUINELY distinctive, EXPLICIT permission to BORROW by ANALOGY from OUTSIDE frameworks, PROVIDED the RESULTING policy remains CONSISTENT with Section 1000'S own CONCEPTS.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Accepted Industry Practice as a Legitimate Reference Point</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A pragmatic acknowledgment of sector-specific convention</div><div class="text-supporting">ACCEPTED industry PRACTICE may ALSO be a LEGITIMATE reference POINT WHERE it PROVIDES a REASONABLE basis for DEVELOPING an ACCOUNTING policy — REFLECTING that CERTAIN sectors HAVE well-established, WIDELY understood CONVENTIONS that MAY reasonably GUIDE policy DEVELOPMENT even ABSENT a SPECIFIC ASPE Section on POINT.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Applying the Hierarchy to an Unaddressed Transaction</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Working through the source hierarchy step by step</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A genuinely novel digital asset transaction</div><div class="wk-body">A private enterprise engages in a transaction involving a genuinely novel type of digital asset with no specific ASPE Section addressing it directly.</div><div class="wk-answer">The company FIRST applies SECTION 1000'S general RECOGNITION and MEASUREMENT concepts. IF this does NOT fully RESOLVE the ISSUE, it MAY then CONSIDER how Part I (IFRS) or US GAAP TREAT similar TRANSACTIONS, DEVELOPING a POLICY BY analogy that REMAINS consistent WITH Section 1000'S own UNDERLYING concepts.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Choosing Between Two Permitted Secondary Sources</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Selecting the most relevant analogy, not simply the most convenient</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Choosing between an IFRS and a US GAAP analogy</div><div class="wk-body">Both IFRS and US GAAP offer a plausible, but differing, treatment for an unaddressed transaction a private enterprise has encountered.</div><div class="wk-answer">The company SELECTS the ANALOGY that MOST FAITHFULLY reflects the ECONOMIC substance of ITS OWN transaction and BEST aligns WITH Section 1000'S UNDERLYING concepts — RATHER than simply CHOOSING whichever TREATMENT produces the MOST favorable financial STATEMENT outcome.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 1100 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Disclosure of the accounting policy developed for a transaction not directly addressed by an existing ASPE Section, per Section 1505</span><span>Basis for the policy selected, where genuinely significant to understanding the financial statements</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether an existing ASPE Section genuinely fails to address a transaction, or whether it applies by reasonable extension</span><span>Which secondary source (IFRS, US GAAP, industry practice) provides the most relevant analogy</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Generally minimal — this Section is a source hierarchy, not a measurement standard itself</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to IAS 8's GAAP Hierarchy</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASPE Section 1100 vs IAS 8's hierarchy</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 1100</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 8 (paras 10–12)</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Standalone Section</span><span>YES — its own dedicated Section</span><span>NO — folded into the broader accounting policies standard</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Permission to reference other frameworks</span><span>Explicit — IFRS, US GAAP, other literature</span><span>Similar in spirit — other standard-setters' pronouncements may be considered</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">CONCEPTUALLY SIMILAR to IAS 8'S own hierarchy for UNADDRESSED issues, but ASPE GIVES this TOPIC its OWN standalone SECTION — a STRUCTURAL, rather than SUBSTANTIVE, point of DIFFERENCE.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where this Section's principles apply</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Concept</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Policy selection for unaddressed transactions</span><span>Varies by transaction</span><span>Determines which statement(s) are affected based on the transaction's nature</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1000</span><span>Financial Statement Concepts — the first point of reference before considering any secondary source</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1505</span><span>Disclosure of Accounting Policies — governs how a policy developed under this Section's hierarchy is disclosed</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">GAAP hierarchy</div><div class="glossary-def">The ordered sequence of authoritative sources an entity consults when developing an accounting policy for a transaction not directly addressed by an existing standard.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Accepted industry practice</div><div class="glossary-def">A widely understood, sector-specific accounting convention that may reasonably guide policy development absent specific ASPE guidance.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe1100 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
